          "question": {
            "text": "Among cellulose, polyvinyl chloride, nylon and natural rubber, the polymer in which the intermolecular force of attraction is weakest in",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "hgC7j",
              "text": "\nnylon\n",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "XVEGP",
              "text": "\npolyvinyl chloride\n",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "KPD4T",
              "text": "\ncellulose\n",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "Dpuv8",
              "text": "\nnatural rubber",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": true
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "$$\n\\text { Nylon has amid linkage capable of forming intermolecular } \\mathrm{H} \\text {-bonding as }\n$$\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/2Z4rUd48WCf_t7lkv_gHa5HajDJtInxtryet1Vuhhl8.original.fullsize.png\"><br>\nDue to $\\mathrm{H}$-bonding, nylon has strong intermolecular attraction.\nCellulose is a polyhydroxy compound, also capable of forming strong intermolecular $\\mathrm{H}$-bonding. Polyvinyl chloride is a polar polymer due to carbon chlorine bond and it possess strong dipole-dipole attraction. Natural rubber is poly (isoprene), a hydrocarbon, possess weak van der Waal's attraction.<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/f5i0Bw1-Ql-QNczMQa41ENvUzZ4r3kWg82G8MZwPurQ.original.fullsize.png\"><br>",
            "image": null
          },
